 "stimulus_html": "<p>“As a final and complete refutation of the charge that lynching is occasioned by crimes against women, a partial record of lynchings is cited; 285 persons were lynched for causes as follows: Unknown cause, 92; no cause, 10; race prejudice, 49; miscegenation, 7; informing, 12; making threats, 11; keeping saloon, 3; practicing fraud, 5; practicing voodooism, 1; refusing evidence, 2; political causes, 5; disputing, 1; disobeying quarantine regulations, 2; slapping a child, 1; turning state’s evidence, 3; protecting a Negro, 1; to prevent giving evidence, 1; knowledge of larceny, 1; writing letter to white woman, 1; asking white woman to marry; 1 . . .</p>\n<p>Is there a remedy, or will the nation confess that it cannot protect its protectors at home as well as abroad? Various remedies have been suggested to abolish the lynching infamy, but year after year, the butchery of men, women and children continues in spite of plea and protest. Education is suggested as a preventive, but it is as grave a crime to murder an ignorant man as it is a scholar. True, few educated men have been lynched, but the hue and cry once started stops at no bounds, as was clearly shown by the lynchings in Atlanta, and in Springfield, Illinois.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">Ida B. Wells, “Lynching, Our National Crime” address at the National Negro Conference, June 1, 1909</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 615690,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "From the excerpt, a historian might conclude Wells believed",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1170728,
     "content_html": "the main reason blacks were lynched was that they had attacked white women"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170729,
     "content_html": "lynching was a national atrocity, not only a Southern atrocity"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170727,
     "content_html": "that because few educated men had been lynched, improving education was an effective solution to the problem"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170726,
     "content_html": "that because the number of lynchings remained high, there was probably nothing Congress could do to address the problem"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615691,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "What would be a logical conclusion from the information Wells provided in the speech excerpted here?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1170732,
     "content_html": "Southern states must provide better security for African Americans in jails."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170731,
     "content_html": "African Americans must apply to fill law enforcement positions so young men in their communities will have good role models."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170733,
     "content_html": "Federal protection of American citizenship is the remedy for lynching."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170730,
     "content_html": "It would be best for African Americans to move to northern states."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615692,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "The viewpoints expressed by Ida B. Wells suggest that popular support for civil rights issues mainly came from",
   "answers": [
